Z||000000||GodSend||03-12-2001||01:24 PM||||Im looking into several home chrome solutions.  I was curious to know what the black grates in the rear deck are made out of (after you remove the paint of course  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.hypernetcommunications.com/revn.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||03-12-2001||01:27 PM||||aluminum.. mine were sand blasted and Powder coated... the stock paint comes off pretty good.. but some is stubborn. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||03-13-2001||09:16 AM||||Don't laugh, but there are spray paints that do a decent job of duplicating chrome look...especially if you put on a few coats of clear coat over them.<P>And they won't be nearly as expensive as chroming or a home "kit".<P>Personally, I prefer the look of black grilles myself, and it's easier to do a decent job with black as you don't have to completely remove all the old paint.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-13-2001||07:25 PM||||I think it depends what version grate it is. I've been told the old style ones are a magnesium alloy or some such thing. The newer ones seem to be something else.<P>Cleaning up the OE paint should be easiest as a chemical job. hit them with the chemical and bag 'em so they don't dry out.<P>If you can get them clean enough you can do whatever you want to them. If they are pitted anytype of shiny finish is going to be a pain to apply and have it look decent.||||reg||1
